        "file_path": "/private/tmp/rp1-evals/a351e089/workspace/.rp1/work/features/yaml-validator-command/design.md",
        "content": "# Design: YAML Validator Command\n\n**Feature ID**: yaml-validator-command\n**Version**: 1.0.0\n**Status**: Draft\n**Created**: 2026-03-01\n\n---\n\n## 1. Design Overview\n\nThe YAML Validator Command adds a `validate` subcommand to the existing CLI entry point (`src/index.ts`). It accepts a single file path, reads the file, runs a from-scratch YAML syntax checker written in TypeScript, and reports whether the file is syntactically valid YAML. On success it prints a confirmation to stdout and exits 0. On failure it prints the file path, line number, and error description to stderr and exits 1. No new dependencies are introduced.\n\n### High-Level Architecture\n\n```mermaid\ngraph LR\n    CLI[\"CLI Entry Point<br/>src/index.ts\"] -->|\"argv[2] = validate\"| VC[\"Validate Command<br/>src/validate-command.ts\"]\n    CLI -->|\"argv[2] != validate\"| GREET[\"Existing Greeting<br/>src/utils.ts\"]\n    VC -->|\"reads file\"| FS[\"File System\"]\n    VC -->|\"passes content\"| YV[\"YAML Validator<br/>src/yaml-validator.ts\"]\n    YV -->|\"returns result\"| VC\n    VC -->|\"success\"| STDOUT[\"stdout (exit 0)\"]\n    VC -->|\"failure\"| STDERR[\"stderr (exit 1)\"]\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2. Architecture\n\n### Component Responsibilities\n\n| Component | File | Responsibility |\n|-----------|------|----------------|\n| CLI Entry Point | `src/index.ts` | Subcommand routing: delegates to validate handler or existing greeting |\n| Validate Command | `src/validate-command.ts` | CLI orchestration: argument extraction, file reading, output formatting, exit codes |\n| YAML Validator | `src/yaml-validator.ts` | Pure YAML syntax checking logic: accepts string, returns structured result |\n| Utilities | `src/utils.ts` | Existing utility functions (unchanged) |\n\n### Sequence Diagram -- Validation Flow\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    participant User\n    participant index as src/index.ts\n    participant cmd as validate-command.ts\n    participant validator as yaml-validator.ts\n    participant fs as File System\n\n    User->>index: bun run dev validate config.yaml\n    index->>index: argv[2] === \"validate\"\n    index->>cmd: runValidateCommand(argv)\n    cmd->>cmd: Extract filePath from argv[3]\n    alt No file path provided\n        cmd->>User: stderr: Usage message (exit 1)\n    else File path provided\n        cmd->>fs: Bun.file(filePath).text()\n        alt File not found / not readable\n            cmd->>User: stderr: File error (exit 1)\n        else File read successfully\n            cmd->>validator: validateYaml(content)\n            validator->>validator: Line-by-line syntax scan\n            validator-->>cmd: ValidationResult\n            alt valid\n                cmd->>User: stdout: \"filePath: valid YAML\" (exit 0)\n            else invalid\n                cmd->>User: stderr: \"filePath:line: message\" (exit 1)\n            end\n        end\n    end\n```\n\n### Subcommand Routing Design\n\nThe entry point (`src/index.ts`) is modified with a simple conditional branch:\n\n```\nif argv[2] === \"validate\" -> import and call runValidateCommand()\nelse -> existing greeting behavior (unchanged)\n```\n\nThis keeps the routing minimal and avoids over-engineering a command framework for a single subcommand. The dynamic import pattern is NOT used; a static import with a conditional call is preferred for simplicity and type safety.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Detailed Design\n\n### 3.1 YAML Validator Module (`src/yaml-validator.ts`)\n\nThis is the core of the feature: a from-scratch YAML syntax checker implemented as a line-by-line scanner with state tracking.\n\n#### Result Type\n\n```typescript\ninterface YamlValidationSuccess {\n  valid: true;\n}\n\ninterface YamlValidationError {\n  valid: false;\n  line: number;\n  message: string;\n}\n\ntype YamlValidationResult = YamlValidationSuccess | YamlValidationError;\n```\n\n#### Exported Function\n\n```typescript\nexport function validateYaml(content: string): YamlValidationResult\n```\n\nA pure function: takes string input, returns a result object. No side effects, no file I/O, no console output.\n\n#### Syntax Checks Implemented\n\nThe checker performs a line-by-line scan with the following checks, listed in order of evaluation:\n\n| Check | Description | Error Example |\n|-------|-------------|---------------|\n| Tab indentation | Tabs used for indentation (YAML forbids this) | `\\tkey: value` |\n| Unclosed quoted strings | Single or double quotes opened but not closed on same line (outside multi-line context) | `key: \"unclosed` |\n| Invalid mapping syntax | Colon in mapping without proper spacing | `key:value` (no space after colon, not a valid scalar) |\n| Unbalanced flow indicators | Unmatched `{`, `}`, `[`, `]` in flow collections | `{key: value` |\n| Duplicate document markers | Multiple `---` or `...` markers in invalid positions | Contextual check |\n| Invalid block scalar headers | Bad characters after `|` or `>` scalar indicators | `|invalid` |\n| Bare invalid characters | Characters not permitted in the current context | Various |\n| Indentation consistency | Dedent to a level that was never established | Contextual check |\n\n#### Design Principles for the Checker\n\n1. **Conservative bias**: When uncertain whether something is valid or invalid, accept it as valid. Minimizing false positives is more important than catching every possible error.\n2. **Line-number accuracy**: The line-by-line approach naturally tracks line numbers (1-indexed).\n3. **Early exit**: Return the first error found. The requirement asks for error details (singular), not a list of all errors.\n4. **State tracking**: Maintain minimal state across lines for context: current indentation stack, whether inside a block scalar, whether inside a flow collection.\n5. **Comment handling**: Lines starting with `#` (after optional whitespace) are skipped. Inline comments (` #`) are stripped before checking.\n\n#### Scope Limitations (Documented)\n\nThe checker does NOT perform:\n- Full YAML 1.2 spec parsing\n- Schema validation\n- Duplicate key detection\n- Anchor/alias resolution\n- Complex multi-line scalar validation beyond basic block scalar headers\n- Tag validation (`!!str`, custom tags)\n\nThese are explicitly out of scope per the requirements.\n\n### 3.2 Validate Command Module (`src/validate-command.ts`)\n\n#### Exported Function\n\n```typescript\nexport async function runValidateCommand(args: string[]): Promise<void>\n```\n\nReceives the full `process.argv` array. Orchestrates:\n\n1. **Argument extraction**: `args[3]` is the file path (index 0 = bun, 1 = script, 2 = \"validate\", 3 = file path).\n2. **Missing argument handling**: If `args[3]` is undefined, print usage to stderr, call `process.exit(1)`.\n3. **File reading**: Use `Bun.file(filePath).text()` (async). Wrap in try/catch for file-not-found and permission errors.\n4. **Validation**: Call `validateYaml(content)`.\n5. **Output formatting**:\n   - Success: `console.log(\"<filePath>: valid YAML\")`\n   - Failure: `console.error(\"<filePath>:<line>: <message>\")`\n6. **Exit code**: `process.exit(0)` for valid, `process.exit(1)` for invalid or error.\n\n#### Error Messages\n\n| Scenario | Output (stderr) | Exit Code |\n|----------|-----------------|-----------|\n| No file path | `Usage: validate <file>` | 1 |\n| File not found | `Error: File not found: <filePath>` | 1 |\n| File not readable | `Error: Cannot read file: <filePath>` | 1 |\n| Invalid YAML | `<filePath>:<line>: <message>` | 1 |\n\n#### Success Message\n\n| Scenario | Output (stdout) | Exit Code |\n|----------|-----------------|-----------|\n| Valid YAML | `<filePath>: valid YAML` | 0 |\n\n### 3.3 Entry Point Modification (`src/index.ts`)\n\nMinimal change to add subcommand routing:\n\n```typescript\nimport { greet } from \"./utils\";\nimport { runValidateCommand } from \"./validate-command\";\n\nconst command = process.argv[2];\n\nif (command === \"validate\") {\n  await runValidateCommand(process.argv);\n} else {\n  const name = command ?? \"World\";\n  console.log(greet(name));\n}\n```\n\nKey points:\n- `await` at top level is supported in Bun ES modules.\n- Existing behavior is fully preserved: any argument that is not `\"validate\"` is treated as a name for greeting.\n- The import of `validate-command` is static (simple, type-safe).\n\n---\n\n## 4. Technology Stack\n\n| Layer | Technology | Justification |\n|-------|-----------|---------------|\n| Runtime | Bun | Existing project runtime |\n| Language | TypeScript (strict) | Existing project language |\n| File I/O | `Bun.file().text()` | Idiomatic Bun file reading API |\n| Testing | `bun:test` | Existing project test framework |\n| YAML Parsing | Custom from-scratch checker | User decision: no external libraries or built-ins |\n\nNo changes to `package.json` dependencies.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Implementation Plan\n\n| ID | Component | File(s) | Description |\n|----|-----------|---------|-------------|\n| T1 | Subcommand Routing | `src/index.ts` | Add conditional branch for \"validate\" subcommand, import validate-command |\n| T2 | YAML Syntax Checker | `src/yaml-validator.ts` | Implement line-by-line YAML syntax validation with state tracking |\n| T3 | Validate Command Handler | `src/validate-command.ts` | CLI orchestration: arg parsing, file I/O, output formatting, exit codes |\n| T4 | Validator Unit Tests | `src/yaml-validator.test.ts` | Test cases for all syntax checks, edge cases, valid files |\n| T5 | Command Integration Tests | `src/validate-command.test.ts` | Test CLI behavior: missing args, bad files, valid/invalid YAML, exit codes |\n\n---\n\n## 6. Implementation DAG\n\n**Parallel Groups** (tasks with no inter-dependencies):\n\n1. [T1, T2] - Independent modules: T1 modifies routing logic, T2 implements pure validation functions. No shared interfaces beyond the import that T1 will use.\n2. [T3, T4] - T3 wires T1+T2 together; T4 tests T2. Both depend on T2 being complete but are independent of each other.\n3. [