d registry pattern -- over-engineered for current scope; (c) CLI framework (e.g., commander) -- would add a dependency, violating FR-006 |\n| D5 | Error output format | Compiler-style `filepath:line: message` | Follows widely-recognized compiler/linter output convention (gcc, eslint, tsc). Developers already understand this format. Enables editor integration (click-to-jump). | (a) Verbose multi-line format -- harder to parse, more visual noise; (b) JSON output -- not human-friendly for a CLI tool; (c) Custom format -- no benefit over established convention |\n| D6 | Checker design philosophy | Conservative bias: accept when uncertain | Minimizing false positives is critical for developer trust. A validator that rejects valid YAML is worse than one that occasionally misses an exotic error. The requirement is \"syntax validation\" for common use cases, not full spec compliance. | (a) Strict checking -- higher false positive risk on valid but unusual YAML constructs; (b) Full spec compliance -- far exceeds scope and effort budget |\n| D7 | Error reporting granularity | First error only (early exit) | The requirements specify error output with \"the line number\" and \"a description\" (singular). Reporting a single actionable error is simpler, clearer, and matches developer expectations for a quick-check tool. | (a) All errors -- more complex output, harder to read, may overwhelm developer; (b) First N errors -- compromise but adds complexity without clear requirement |\n| D8 | Top-level await in index.ts | Use top-level await for async validate command | Bun ES modules support top-level await natively. This keeps the entry point clean without wrapping in an async IIFE. | (a) Async IIFE wrapper -- unnecessary boilerplate in Bun; (b) Make validate command synchronous -- would require sync file reading, less idiomatic |\n| D9 | Exit code strategy | Exit 0 for valid, exit 1 for all other cases (invalid, error, missing arg) | Standard UNIX convention. Single non-zero code (