        "content": "# Requirements Specification: YAML Validator Command\n\n**Feature ID**: yaml-validator-command\n**Version**: 1.0.0\n**Status**: Draft\n**Created**: 2026-03-01\n\n## 1. Feature Overview\n\nThe project needs a built-in YAML validation capability exposed as a subcommand of the existing CLI entry point. Developers working on the project need a quick way to check whether a given YAML file is syntactically well-formed, receiving clear pass/fail feedback with actionable error details when validation fails. This must be accomplished without introducing any new external dependencies.\n\n## 2. Business Context\n\n### 2.1 Problem Statement\n\nDevelopers on this project currently have no integrated way to verify that YAML files are syntactically correct before using them. Malformed YAML can lead to silent failures, misconfiguration, or wasted debugging time. An external tool or manual inspection is required today, which breaks the developer workflow and introduces inconsistency in how validation is performed across the team.\n\n### 2.2 Business Value\n\n- Reduces developer time spent debugging YAML syntax errors by providing immediate, in-project feedback.\n- Standardizes YAML validation across all project contributors using a single consistent tool.\n- Keeps the project dependency footprint minimal by relying only on capabilities already available in the runtime environment.\n- Lowers the barrier to catching configuration errors early in the development cycle.\n\n### 2.3 Success Metrics\n\n| Metric | Target |\n|--------|--------|\n| Validation accuracy | Correctly identifies all syntactically invalid YAML files and passes all valid ones |\n| Error clarity | Error output includes the line number and a human-readable description of the syntax problem |\n| Developer adoption | Team members use the command instead of external tools for YAML validation |\n| Dependency count change | Zero new dependencies added to package.json |\n\n## 3. Stakeholders & Users\n\n### 3.1 User Types\n\n| User Type | Description | Relevance |\n|-----------|-------------|-----------|\n| Project Developer | A developer actively working on this project who authors or modifies YAML files | Primary user; runs the command during development to validate YAML files before committing or using them |\n\n### 3.2 Stakeholder Interests\n\n| Stakeholder | Interest |\n|-------------|----------|\n| Project Developers | Fast, reliable syntax checking integrated into their existing workflow |\n| Project Maintainers | No new dependencies to audit, maintain, or update; minimal surface area for the feature |\n\n## 4. Scope Definition\n\n### 4.1 In Scope\n\n- A `validate` subcommand added to the existing CLI entry point (`src/index.ts`).\n- Accepting a single file path as a command-line argument.\n- Parsing the file content as YAML to determine syntactic validity.\n- Reporting a clear \"valid\" result when the file is well-formed YAML.\n- Reporting a clear \"invalid\" result with line number and error description when the file is malformed.\n- Using an appropriate exit code to indicate success (0) or failure (non-zero).\n- Operating without any new runtime or dev dependencies beyond what is already in `package.json`.\n\n### 4.2 Out of Scope\n\n- Schema validation (validating YAML structure against a schema definition).\n- Structural checks beyond syntax (e.g., duplicate key detection, semantic analysis).\n- Accepting input from standard input (stdin/piped content).\n- Validating multiple files in a single invocation (glob patterns or multiple arguments).\n- A separate script or entry point (the command must be a subcommand of the existing entry point).\n- Any YAML writing, formatting, or transformation capabilities.\n- Interactive or watch-mode validation.\n- Integration with CI/CD pipelines (though the exit code behavior does not preclude future use there).\n\n### 4.3 Assumptions\n\n- The Bun runtime environment provides built-in YAML parsing capability or the project can implement basic YAML syntax checking without external libraries.\n- Developers invoke the CLI via `bun run dev` as documented in the project README.\n- YAML files to be validated exist on the local filesystem and are readable by the process.\n- The expected invocation pattern is: `bun run dev validate <file-path>`.\n\n## 5. Functional Requirements\n\n### FR-001: Subcommand Recognition\n\n- **Priority**: Must Have\n- **User Type**: Project Developer\n- **Requirement**: When a developer runs the CLI entry point with `validate` as the first argument, the system must recognize it as the YAML validation subcommand and route execution accordingly.\n- **Rationale**: The command must be discoverable and consistent with the existing CLI structure so developers do not need to learn a separate tool.\n- **Acceptance Criteria**:\n  - AC-001a: Running `bun run dev validate <file>` invokes the YAML validation logic.\n  - AC-001b: Running `bun run dev` without `validate` preserves the existing greeting behavior.\n\n### FR-002: File Path Argument\n\n- **Priority**: Must Have\n- **User Type**: Project Developer\n- **Requirement**: The validate subcommand must accept exactly one positional argument representing the file path of the YAML file to validate.\n- **Rationale**: Developers need to specify which file to check; a single-file interface keeps the feature simple and predictable.\n- **Acceptance Criteria**:\n  - AC-002a: When a valid file path is provided, the system reads and validates that file.\n  - AC-002b: When no file path is provided, the system displays a usage message indicating that a file path is required.\n  - AC-002c: When the specified file does not exist or is not readable, the system displays a clear error message stating the file cannot be found or read.\n\n### FR-003: Syntax Validation\n\n- **Priority**: Must Have\n- **User Type**: Project Developer\n- **Requirement**: The system must parse the provided file as YAML and determine whether it is syntactically well-formed.\n- **Rationale**: Syntax validation is the core purpose of the feature, allowing developers to catch malformed YAML before it causes downstream issues.\n- **Acceptance Criteria**:\n  - AC-003a: A syntactically valid YAML file produces a \"valid\" result.\n  - AC-003b: A syntactically invalid YAML file produces an \"invalid\" result.\n  - AC-003c: An empty file is treated as valid YAML (an empty document is valid YAML per the specification).\n\n### FR-004: Success Output\n\n- **Priority**: Must Have\n- **User Type**: Project Developer\n- **Requirement**: When validation succeeds, the system must print a clear confirmation message to standard output indicating the file is valid YAML.\n- **Rationale**: Developers need positive confirmation that their file passed validation, not just the absence of errors.\n- **Acceptance Criteria**:\n  - AC-004a: The output message includes the file path and an indication that the file is valid.\n  - AC-004b: The process exits with code 0.\n\n### FR-005: Failure Output with Error Details\n\n- **Priority**: Must Have\n- **User Type**: Project Developer\n- **Requirement**: When validation fails, the system must print an error message to standard error that includes the file path, the line number where the error was detected, and a human-readable description of the syntax problem.\n- **Rationale**: Developers need actionable information to locate and fix the problem quickly, without having to open the file and guess where the issue is.\n- **Acceptance Criteria**:\n  - AC-005a: The error output includes the file path.\n  - AC-005b: The error output includes the line number of the syntax error.\n  - AC-005c: The error output includes a human-readable description of what is wrong.\n  - AC-005d: The process exits with a non-zero exit code.\n\n### FR-006: No New Dependencies\n\n- **Priority**: Must Have\n- **User Type**: Project Maintainer\n- **Requirement**: The YAML validation feature must be implemented using only the capabilities already available in the project runtime and its existing dependencies. No new entries may be added to `dependencies` or `devDependencies` in `package.json`.\n- **Rationale**: The explicit constraint from the requirements is to avoid adding new dependencies, keeping the project lean and reducing maintenance burden.\n- **Acceptance Criteria**:\n  - AC-006a: The `package.json` file has no new entries in `dependencies` or `devDependencies` after the feature is implemented.\n  - AC-006b: No new `node_modules` packages are required at runtime for the feature to function.\n\n## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n\n### 6.1 Performance Expectations\n\n| Expectation | Target |\n|-------------|--------|\n| Validation speed | Validation of a typical YAML file (under 1000 lines) should complete in under 1 second |\n| Startup overhead | The subcommand should not add noticeable latency to the CLI startup for other commands |\n\n### 6.2 Security Requirements\n\n| Requirement | Description |\n|-------------|-------------|\n| File access | The command must only read the file specified by the user; it must not write to or modify any files |\n| Path traversal | The command should handle file paths as provided without special sanitization, relying on OS-level file permissions for access control |\n\n### 6.3 Usability Requirements\n\n| Requirement | Description |\n|-------------|-------------|\n| Error clarity | Error messages must be understandable by a developer without needing to consult external documentation |\n| Consistent interface | The subcommand must follow the same invocation pattern as the existing CLI (`bun run dev <subcommand> <args>`) |\n| Missing argument guidance | When invoked without a file path, the command must display usage instructions |\n\n### 6.4 Compliance Requirements\n\nNo specific regulatory or compliance requirements apply to this feature.\n\n## 7. User Stories\n\n### STORY-001: Validate a well-formed YAML file\n\n**As a** project developer,\n**I want to** run a validation command against a YAML file I have written,\n**So that** I can confirm it is syntactically correct before using it.\n\n**Acceptance Scenarios**:\n\n- **GIVEN** a file `config.yaml` that contains valid YAML syntax,\n  **WHEN** the developer runs `bun run dev validate config.yaml`,\n  **THEN** the system prints a message confirming the file is valid and exits with code 0.\n\n### STORY-002: Identify syntax errors in a malformed YAML file\n\n**As a** project developer,\n**I want to** see the exact line number and description of a YAML syntax error,\n**So that** I can quickly locate and fix the problem without manually scanning the file.\n\n**Acceptance Scenarios**:\n\n- **GIVEN** a file `broken.yaml` that contains a syntax error on line 5,\n  **WHEN** the developer runs `bun run dev validate broken.yaml`,\n  **THEN** the system prints an error message that includes the file name, line number 5, and a description of the syntax issue, and exits with a non-zero code.\n\n### STORY-003: Handle missing file path argument\n\n**As a** project developer,\n**I want to** see a usage hint when I forget to provide a file path,\n**So that** I know the correct way to invoke the command.\n\n**Acceptance Scenarios**:\n\n- **GIVEN** the developer runs `bun run dev validate` with no file argument,\n  **WHEN** the command is executed,\n  **THEN** the system prints a usage message (e.g., \"Usage: validate <file>\") and exits with a non-zero code.\n\n### STORY-004: Handle non-existent file\n\n**As a** project developer,\n**I want to** see a clear error when I specify a file that does not exist,\n**So that** I can correct the file path without confusion.\n\n**Acceptance Scenarios**:\n\n- **GIVEN** the developer runs `bun run dev validate nonexistent.yaml`,\n  **WHEN** the file does not exist on disk,\n  **THEN** the system prints an error message indicating the file was not found and exits with a non-zero code.\n\n### STORY-005: Existing CLI behavior is preserved\n\n**As a** project developer,\n**I want** the existing greeting behavior to continue working when I do not use the validate subcommand,\n**So that** the new feature does not break anything.\n\n**Acceptance Scenarios**:\n\n- **GIVEN** the developer runs `bun run dev Alice`,\n  **WHEN** the first argument is not `validate`,\n  **THEN** the system prints \"Hello, Alice!\" as before.\n\n## 8. Business Rules\n\n| Rule ID | Rule | Rationale |\n|---------|------|-----------|\n| BR-001 | The validate command must not modify any file on disk | Validation is a read-only operation; any mutation would be unexpected and potentially destructive |\n| BR-002 | An empty file is considered valid YAML | Per the YAML specification, an empty document is valid; the validator should not produce false negatives for empty files |\n| BR-003 | The command must not introduce new dependencies | Explicit project constraint to keep the dependency footprint unchanged |\n| BR-004 | Exit code 0 means valid; non-zero means invalid or error | Standard CLI convention that enables future use in scripts and CI pipelines |\n\n## 9. Dependencies & Constraints\n\n| Type | Description |\n|------|-------------|\n| Runtime Dependency | Bun runtime (already present) must provide or enable YAML parsing without external packages |\n| Existing Codebase | The entry point `src/index.ts` must be extended to support subcommand routing; changes must not break existing behavior |\n| No New Dependencies | The `package.json` must remain unchanged in its dependency sections |\n| File System | Validated files must exist on the local filesystem and be readable by the running process |\n\n## 10. Clarifications Log\n\n| Question | Answer | Source |\n|----------|--------|--------|\n| Who is the target user? | Developers on this project | User clarification |\n| How is YAML input provided? | File path argument only (e.g., `bun run dev validate config.yaml`) | User clarification |\n| What depth of validation? | Syntax validation only (well-formedness check) | User clarification |\n| What should the output include? | Pass/fail with error details including line numbers | User clarification |\n| Single or multiple files? | Single file only | User clarification |\n| How does the command integrate? | Subcommand of the existing entry point | User clarification |\n\n## AFK Mode: Auto-Selected Defaults\n\n| Decision Point | Choice | Rationale |\n|----------------|--------|-----------|\n| PRD association | None | No PRD files found in `.rp1/work/prds/`; feature proceeds without parent PRD |\n| KB context | Best-effort from codebase | No `index.md`, `concept_map.md`, or `charter.md` found; used direct codebase inspection for project understanding |\n| Empty file validity | Valid YAML | YAML specification defines empty documents as valid; conservative default avoids false negatives |\n| Error output destination | stderr for errors, stdout for success | Standard CLI convention; keeps machine-parseable output clean |\n| Exit code semantics | 0 = valid, 1 = invalid/error | Standard UNIX convention for CLI tools |\n\n## AFK Mode: Inferred Decisions\n\n| Ambiguity | Resolution | Source |\n|-----------|------------|--------|\n| Which YAML parsing capability to rely on | Bun runtime built-in or standard JavaScript-accessible parsing | Codebase inspection: project uses Bun runtime with no current YAML dependencies; constraint prohibits new deps |\n| Subcommand routing mechanism | Extend existing `process.argv` handling in `src/index.ts` | Codebase inspection: current entry point already uses `process.argv[2]` for input |\n| Behavior for non-YAML file extensions | Attempt validation regardless of extension | Conservative default: file extension is not a reliable indicator of content; validate actual content |\n| Behavior for binary files | Report as invalid YAML with appropriate error | Conservative default: binary content will fail YAML parsing and be reported as a syntax error |\n| Usage message format | Simple one-line usage hint | Codebase inspection: project is minimal; a lightweight usage message fits the project style |\n"
