        "content": "# Design Decisions: YAML Validator Command\n\n**Feature ID**: yaml-validator-command\n**Created**: 2026-03-01\n\n## Decision Log\n\n| ID | Decision | Choice | Rationale | Alternatives Considered |\n|----|----------|--------|-----------|------------------------|\n| D1 | YAML parsing approach | From-scratch TypeScript syntax checker | User explicitly decided: no external libraries or Bun built-ins. A hand-written line-by-line scanner covers common syntax errors while satisfying the zero-dependency constraint. | (a) Bun built-in YAML parser -- uncertain availability, user rejected; (b) Vendored js-yaml -- violates spirit of no-dependency constraint, user rejected; (c) Full spec-compliant parser from scratch -- excessive scope for syntax-only validation |\n| D2 | Module structure | Three separate modules: yaml-validator.ts (pure logic), validate-command.ts (CLI orchestration), index.ts (routing) | Follows existing codebase pattern where pure functions live in dedicated modules (like utils.ts) and index.ts is thin wiring. Separation enables unit testing of validation logic without CLI side effects. | (a) All logic in index.ts -- violates existing separation pattern, untestable; (b) Two modules (validator + index.ts) -- mixes CLI orchestration with pure logic |\n| D3 | File reading API | `Bun.file(filePath).text()` | Idiomatic Bun API for file reading. Async, returns string directly. Consistent with the project's Bun-first approach. | (a) `fs.readFileSync()` -- Node.js compat layer, synchronous, less idiomatic for Bun; (b) `fs.promises.readFile()` -- works but less idiomatic than Bun native API |\n| D4 | Subcommand routing mechanism | Simple if/else conditional in index.ts | The project has exactly one subcommand (validate) plus the default greeting. A full command framework would be over-engineered. If/else matches the existing simplicity of the codebase. | (a) Switch statement -- marginally more extensible but unnecessary for two branches; (b) Command registry pattern -- over-engineered for current scope; (c) CLI framework (e.g., commander) -- would add a dependency, violating FR-006 |\n| D5 | Error output format | Compiler-style `filepath:line: message` | Follows widely-recognized compiler/linter output convention (gcc, eslint, tsc). Developers already understand this format. Enables editor integration (click-to-jump). | (a) Verbose multi-line format -- harder to parse, more visual noise; (b) JSON output -- not human-friendly for a CLI tool; (c) Custom format -- no benefit over established convention |\n| D6 | Checker design philosophy | Conservative bias: accept when uncertain | Minimizing false positives is critical for developer trust. A validator that rejects valid YAML is worse than one that occasionally misses an exotic error. The requirement is \"syntax validation\" for common use cases, not full spec compliance. | (a) Strict checking -- higher false positive risk on valid but unusual YAML constructs; (b) Full spec compliance -- far exceeds scope and effort budget |\n| D7 | Error reporting granularity | First error only (early exit) | The requirements specify error output with \"the line number\" and \"a description\" (singular). Reporting a single actionable error is simpler, clearer, and matches developer expectations for a quick-check tool. | (a) All errors -- more complex output, harder to read, may overwhelm developer; (b) First N errors -- compromise but adds complexity without clear requirement |\n| D8 | Top-level await in index.ts | Use top-level await for async validate command | Bun ES modules support top-level await natively. This keeps the entry point clean without wrapping in an async IIFE. | (a) Async IIFE wrapper -- unnecessary boilerplate in Bun; (b) Make validate command synchronous -- would require sync file reading, less idiomatic |\n| D9 | Exit code strategy | Exit 0 for valid, exit 1 for all other cases (invalid, error, missing arg) | Standard UNIX convention. Single non-zero code (1) is sufficient since the error message differentiates between \"invalid YAML\" and \"file not found\". No need for distinct error codes. | (a) Distinct exit codes (1=invalid, 2=file error, 3=usage error) -- over-specified for current requirements, adds complexity |\n| D10 | Test approach for CLI integration | Direct function calls + Bun.spawn for true CLI tests | Unit tests call exported functions directly for speed. A small number of integration tests use Bun.spawn to verify the actual CLI behavior end-to-end (exit codes, stdout/stderr). | (a) All tests via Bun.spawn -- slower, harder to debug; (b) All tests via function calls -- misses real CLI integration issues (exit codes, output streams) |\n\n## AFK Mode: Auto-Selected Technology Decisions\n\n| Decision | Choice | Source | Rationale |\n|----------|--------|--------|-----------|\n| File reading API | `Bun.file().text()` | Codebase (Bun runtime) | Idiomatic Bun file I/O; project is Bun-native with bun-types |\n| Subcommand routing | if/else in index.ts | Codebase pattern | Matches existing minimal routing via process.argv[2]; only two branches needed |\n| Error output destination | stderr for errors/invalid, stdout for valid | CLI convention | Standard UNIX practice; keeps success output pipeable |\n| Test framework | bun:test (describe/test/expect) | Codebase (utils.test.ts) | Existing test pattern in project; no new test dependencies |\n| Test file location | Co-located in src/ | Codebase (utils.test.ts) | Follows existing pattern of test files alongside source |\n| Success message format | `<filepath>: valid YAML` | Conservative default | Includes filepath per AC-004a; concise and informative |\n| Error message format | `<filepath>:<line>: <message>` | CLI convention | Standard compiler-style format familiar to developers |\n| Empty file handling | Treated as valid | Requirement BR-002 | YAML spec: empty document is valid; explicitly required |\n| Argument index for file path | `process.argv[3]` | Codebase pattern | argv[0]=bun, argv[1]=script, argv[2]=\"validate\", argv[3]=filepath |\n| Module naming convention | kebab-case filenames | Codebase pattern | Consistent with existing src/ file naming |\n"
