        "content": "# Requirements Specification: YAML Validator Command\n\n**Feature ID**: yaml-validator-command\n**Version**: 1.0.0\n**Status**: Draft\n**Created**: 2026-03-01\n\n## 1. Feature Overview\n\nThe project needs a command-line capability that allows developers to validate YAML files for syntactic correctness directly from the existing CLI tooling. The command must be implemented without introducing any new external dependencies, relying solely on capabilities already available in the project runtime environment.\n\n## 2. Business Context\n\n### 2.1 Problem Statement\n\nDevelopers working with YAML configuration files currently have no built-in way to check whether a YAML file is syntactically valid before it is consumed by downstream processes. Invalid YAML can cause runtime errors, failed deployments, or misconfigured systems that are costly and time-consuming to debug.\n\n### 2.2 Business Value\n\n- Reduces developer time spent debugging malformed YAML files.\n- Catches syntax errors early in the development workflow, before they propagate to more expensive stages (build, deploy, runtime).\n- Keeps the project dependency footprint minimal by not requiring additional packages, reducing supply-chain risk and maintenance burden.\n\n### 2.3 Success Metrics\n\n| Metric | Target |\n|--------|--------|\n| Developers can validate a YAML file in a single command invocation | 100% of valid/invalid files produce correct pass/fail result |\n| No new entries added to package.json dependencies or devDependencies (beyond existing) | Zero new dependencies |\n| Command provides actionable error output for invalid YAML | Error messages include line/position information when available |\n\n## 3. Stakeholders & Users\n\n### 3.1 User Types\n\n| User Type | Description | Relevance |\n|-----------|-------------|-----------|\n| Developer | A developer working on or with this project who authors or edits YAML files | Primary user of the validator command |\n| CI/CD Pipeline Operator | An automated system or person configuring CI pipelines that may invoke the validator as a quality gate | Secondary consumer of the command |\n\n### 3.2 Stakeholder Interests\n\n| Stakeholder | Interest |\n|-------------|----------|\n| Project Maintainers | Keep the dependency footprint minimal; maintain project simplicity |\n| Developers | Fast, reliable feedback on YAML file correctness |\n\n## 4. Scope Definition\n\n### 4.1 In Scope\n\n- A CLI command that accepts a path to a YAML file and reports whether it is syntactically valid.\n- Clear, human-readable output indicating success or failure.\n- Actionable error messages for invalid YAML (including error description and position if available).\n- Non-zero exit code on validation failure (to support scripting and CI usage).\n- Zero exit code on validation success.\n\n### 4.2 Out of Scope\n\n- YAML schema validation (validating against a specific structure or data contract).\n- Batch validation of entire directories in a single invocation (may be a future enhancement).\n- Auto-fixing or auto-formatting of invalid YAML.\n- Support for non-YAML formats (JSON, TOML, etc.).\n- GUI or web-based validation interface.\n- Adding any new runtime or development dependencies to the project.\n\n### 4.3 Assumptions\n\n- The Bun runtime environment provides sufficient built-in capability to parse YAML syntax without external libraries.\n- YAML files to be validated are accessible on the local filesystem.\n- The command will be invoked manually by developers or by scripts/CI pipelines.\n\n## 5. Functional Requirements\n\n### FR-001: Single File Validation\n\n- **Priority**: Must Have\n- **User Type**: Developer\n- **Requirement**: A developer must be able to invoke a command with a file path argument and receive a clear pass/fail result indicating whether the specified file contains syntactically valid YAML.\n- **Rationale**: This is the core purpose of the feature -- providing a quick syntax check for individual YAML files.\n- **Acceptance Criteria**:\n  - AC-1: Given a path to a syntactically valid YAML file, the command outputs a success message and exits with code 0.\n  - AC-2: Given a path to a syntactically invalid YAML file, the command outputs a failure message with error details and exits with a non-zero exit code.\n\n### FR-002: Actionable Error Reporting\n\n- **Priority**: Must Have\n- **User Type**: Developer\n- **Requirement**: When validation fails, the command must provide an error message that describes the nature of the syntax error and, when available, the location (line number or character position) of the error within the file.\n- **Rationale**: Developers need to know not just that a file is invalid, but where and why, to efficiently correct the problem.\n- **Acceptance Criteria**:\n  - AC-1: The error output includes a human-readable description of the syntax problem.\n  - AC-2: The error output includes line number or position information when the underlying parser provides it.\n\n### FR-003: File Not Found Handling\n\n- **Priority**: Must Have\n- **User Type**: Developer\n- **Requirement**: When the specified file path does not exist or is not readable, the command must report a clear error indicating the file could not be found or accessed, and exit with a non-zero exit code.\n- **Rationale**: Distinguishing between \"file not found\" and \"invalid YAML\" prevents confusion and wasted debugging time.\n- **Acceptance Criteria**:\n  - AC-1: Given a non-existent file path, the command outputs a \"file not found\" (or equivalent) error message.\n  - AC-2: The exit code is non-zero.\n\n### FR-004: Missing Argument Handling\n\n- **Priority**: Must Have\n- **User Type**: Developer\n- **Requirement**: When the command is invoked without a file path argument, it must display a brief usage message explaining the expected input.\n- **Rationale**: Provides a self-documenting experience so developers can quickly understand how to use the command without consulting external documentation.\n- **Acceptance Criteria**:\n  - AC-1: Invoking the command with no arguments outputs a usage/help message.\n  - AC-2: The exit code is non-zero.\n\n### FR-005: Zero New Dependencies\n\n- **Priority**: Must Have\n- **User Type**: Project Maintainer\n- **Requirement**: The YAML validator command must be implemented using only capabilities already present in the project's runtime environment and existing dependency set. No new entries may be added to package.json dependencies or devDependencies.\n- **Rationale**: The explicit constraint from the requirements is to avoid new dependencies, preserving the minimal footprint and reducing supply-chain risk.\n- **Acceptance Criteria**:\n  - AC-1: The package.json file has no new dependency entries after implementation compared to before.\n  - AC-2: No new package-lock, bun.lockb, or equivalent lock file entries are introduced for new packages.\n\n### FR-006: Exit Code Conventions\n\n- **Priority**: Should Have\n- **User Type**: CI/CD Pipeline Operator\n- **Requirement**: The command should follow standard CLI exit code conventions: 0 for success, 1 for validation failure, and a distinct non-zero code (e.g., 2) for usage errors (missing arguments, file not found).\n- **Rationale**: Standard exit codes enable reliable integration with shell scripts, CI pipelines, and other automation tooling.\n- **Acceptance Criteria**:\n  - AC-1: Exit code 0 is returned on successful validation.\n  - AC-2: A non-zero exit code is returned on validation failure.\n  - AC-3: A non-zero exit code is returned on usage errors.\n\n## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n\n### 6.1 Performance Expectations\n\n| Expectation | Detail |\n|-------------|--------|\n| Response time | Validation of a typical YAML file (under 1 MB) should complete in under 1 second |\n| Resource usage | The command should not consume excessive memory; processing a single file should not require loading more than the file itself plus reasonable parsing overhead |\n\n### 6.2 Security Requirements\n\n| Requirement | Detail |\n|-------------|--------|\n| File access | The command should only read the file specified by the user; it must not write to or modify any files |\n| Input handling | The command must not execute or evaluate any content within the YAML file; it only checks syntax |\n\n### 6.3 Usability Requirements\n\n| Requirement | Detail |\n|-------------|--------|\n| Output clarity | Success and failure messages must be immediately understandable without consulting documentation |\n| Discoverability | A usage/help message must be shown when invoked incorrectly |\n| Consistency | Output format and exit codes should be consistent across invocations |\n\n### 6.4 Compliance Requirements\n\nNo specific regulatory or compliance requirements apply to this feature.\n\n## 7. User Stories\n\n### STORY-001: Validate a correct YAML file\n\n- **As a** developer\n- **I want to** run a validation command against a YAML file that I believe is correct\n- **So that** I can confirm it is syntactically valid before using it in my workflow\n\n**Acceptance Test**:\n- GIVEN a YAML file with valid syntax\n- WHEN the developer runs the validate command with the file path\n- THEN the command outputs a success message and exits with code 0\n\n### STORY-002: Identify errors in an invalid YAML file\n\n- **As a** developer\n- **I want to** run a validation command against a YAML file that contains syntax errors\n- **So that** I can see what is wrong and where the error is located\n\n**Acceptance Test**:\n- GIVEN a YAML file with a syntax error (e.g., incorrect indentation, missing colon)\n- WHEN the developer runs the validate command with the file path\n- THEN the command outputs an error message describing the problem and its location, and exits with a non-zero code\n\n### STORY-003: Handle missing file gracefully\n\n- **As a** developer\n- **I want to** receive a clear error when I accidentally provide a wrong file path\n- **So that** I can correct the path without confusion about whether the YAML itself was invalid\n\n**Acceptance Test**:\n- GIVEN a file path that does not exist on the filesystem\n- WHEN the developer runs the validate command with that path\n- THEN the command outputs a \"file not found\" error and exits with a non-zero code\n\n### STORY-004: Learn command usage\n\n- **As a** developer unfamiliar with the command\n- **I want to** see usage instructions when I invoke the command without arguments\n- **So that** I can learn how to use it without searching for external documentation\n\n**Acceptance Test**:\n- GIVEN the developer invokes the validate command with no arguments\n- WHEN the command runs\n- THEN it displays a usage message showing the expected argument format and exits with a non-zero code\n\n### STORY-005: Use validator in CI pipeline\n\n- **As a** CI/CD pipeline operator\n- **I want to** include the YAML validation command as a step in an automated pipeline\n- **So that** invalid YAML configuration files are caught before deployment\n\n**Acceptance Test**:\n- GIVEN a CI pipeline step that invokes the validate command on a YAML file\n- WHEN the file is valid, THEN the step passes (exit code 0)\n- WHEN the file is invalid, THEN the step fails (non-zero exit code)\n\n## 8. Business Rules\n\n| Rule ID | Rule | Rationale |\n|---------|------|-----------|\n| BR-001 | The command must not add any new dependencies to the project | Explicit stakeholder constraint to maintain a minimal dependency footprint |\n| BR-002 | The command must be read-only; it must never modify the input file | Validation is an inspection activity and must not have side effects |\n| BR-003 | The command must distinguish between file-access errors and YAML syntax errors in its output | Different error categories require different corrective actions from the user |\n\n## 9. Dependencies & Constraints\n\n| Dependency/Constraint | Type | Detail |\n|-----------------------|------|--------|\n| Bun runtime | Runtime dependency | The project uses Bun as its runtime; the validator must work within this environment |\n| No new dependencies | Hard constraint | The requirement explicitly forbids adding new packages |\n| Existing project structure | Soft constraint | The command should follow the existing project conventions (TypeScript, src/ directory, ESM modules) |\n| YAML parsing capability | Capability dependency | The implementation depends on the runtime providing or enabling YAML parsing without external packages |\n\n## 10. Clarifications Log\n\nNo interactive clarifications were requested (AFK mode was active). All ambiguities were resolved via inference. See the AFK Mode sections below for details.\n\n## AFK Mode: Auto-Selected Defaults\n\n| Decision Point | Choice | Rationale |\n|----------------|--------|-----------|\n| PRD selection | No PRD associated | No PRDs exist in the workspace; no association possible |\n| Validation scope | Syntax validation only (no schema validation) | Conservative default given \"simple\" requirement and no-new-deps constraint |\n| Target file count per invocation | Single file per invocation | \"Simple\" requirement suggests minimal scope; batch processing deferred to future enhancement |\n| Error output format | Plain text to stderr | Standard CLI convention; no requirement suggests structured output (JSON, etc.) |\n| Exit code scheme | 0 = success, non-zero = failure | Standard CLI convention; specific codes left to implementation |\n\n## AFK Mode: Inferred Decisions\n\n| Ambiguity | Resolution | Source |\n|-----------|------------|--------|\n| \"simple\" -- vague scope qualifier | Interpreted as: single-file validation, syntax-only, clear pass/fail output, minimal feature surface | Conservative default; consistent with no-new-deps constraint |\n| \"yaml validator\" -- no actor specified | Primary actor is a Developer using the CLI; secondary actor is CI/CD automation | Inferred from project context (CLI tool, developer-focused project) |\n| \"command\" -- unclear integration point | A new CLI entry point (script) following existing project patterns (Bun script in src/) | Inferred from existing project structure (src/index.ts pattern, package.json scripts) |\n| \"without adding new deps\" -- scope of constraint | Applies to both dependencies and devDependencies in package.json; no new npm packages of any kind | Literal interpretation of requirement |\n| Depth of validation | Syntax/parse validation only; no structural or schema validation | Conservative default given \"simple\" qualifier and dependency constraint |\n"
