        "content": "# Design: YAML Validator Command\n\n**Feature ID**: yaml-validator-command\n**Version**: 1.0.0\n**Status**: Draft\n**Created**: 2026-03-01\n\n---\n\n## 1. Design Overview\n\nThis feature adds a CLI command to the existing Bun/TypeScript project that validates a single YAML file for syntactic correctness. The design follows the project's existing patterns: a new entry-point script in `src/`, a utility module with pure functions, colocated tests, and a `package.json` script for invocation. No new dependencies are introduced; the implementation relies on Bun's built-in YAML parsing capability.\n\n### High-Level Architecture\n\n```mermaid\ngraph LR\n    A[Developer / CI] -->|\"bun run validate-yaml <file>\"| B[\"src/validate-yaml.ts<br/>(CLI Entry Point)\"]\n    B -->|reads args| C[\"process.argv\"]\n    B -->|reads file| D[\"Filesystem<br/>(Bun.file)\"]\n    B -->|delegates parsing| E[\"src/yaml-validator.ts<br/>(Validation Logic)\"]\n    E -->|parses YAML| F[\"Bun Built-in<br/>YAML Parser\"]\n    B -->|outputs result| G[\"stdout / stderr\"]\n    B -->|sets exit code| H[\"process.exit()\"]\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2. Architecture\n\nThe feature introduces two new source files and follows the existing two-layer pattern observed in the codebase (entry point + utility module).\n\n### Component Responsibilities\n\n| Component | File | Responsibility |\n|-----------|------|----------------|\n| CLI Entry Point | `src/validate-yaml.ts` | Argument parsing, file I/O, output formatting, exit code management |\n| Validation Logic | `src/yaml-validator.ts` | Pure YAML parsing/validation functions, error extraction |\n| Tests | `src/yaml-validator.test.ts` | Unit tests for validation logic |\n\n### Sequence Diagram: Validation Flow\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    participant User as Developer/CI\n    participant CLI as validate-yaml.ts\n    participant Val as yaml-validator.ts\n    participant FS as Filesystem\n    participant Parser as Bun YAML Parser\n\n    User->>CLI: bun run validate-yaml <path>\n    CLI->>CLI: Parse process.argv\n\n    alt No file argument\n        CLI->>User: Usage message (stderr)\n        CLI->>CLI: process.exit(2)\n    end\n\n    CLI->>FS: Check file exists (Bun.file)\n    alt File not found\n        CLI->>User: \"File not found\" error (stderr)\n        CLI->>CLI: process.exit(2)\n    end\n\n    CLI->>FS: Read file contents\n    CLI->>Val: validateYaml(contents)\n    Val->>Parser: Parse YAML string\n    alt Valid YAML\n        Parser-->>Val: Parsed successfully\n        Val-->>CLI: { valid: true }\n        CLI->>User: Success message (stdout)\n        CLI->>CLI: process.exit(0)\n    else Invalid YAML\n        Parser-->>Val: Throws parse error\n        Val-->>CLI: { valid: false, error: { message, line?, column? } }\n        CLI->>User: Error details (stderr)\n        CLI->>CLI: process.exit(1)\n    end\n```\n\n---\n\n## 3. Detailed Design\n\n### 3.1 Validation Logic Module (`src/yaml-validator.ts`)\n\nThis module contains pure functions with no side effects, following the pattern of `src/utils.ts`.\n\n**Exported Types:**\n\n```typescript\n/** Result of a YAML validation attempt. */\nexport interface YamlValidationResult {\n  valid: boolean;\n  error?: {\n    message: string;\n    line?: number;\n    column?: number;\n  };\n}\n```\n\n**Exported Functions:**\n\n```typescript\n/**\n * Validate a string as syntactically correct YAML.\n * Returns a result object indicating success or failure with error details.\n */\nexport function validateYaml(content: string): YamlValidationResult;\n```\n\n**Implementation approach:**\n- Use Bun's built-in YAML parsing capability to attempt a parse of the input string.\n- Wrap the parse call in a try/catch.\n- On success, return `{ valid: true }`.\n- On failure, extract the error message and any available position information (line, column) from the caught error and return a structured result.\n\n### 3.2 CLI Entry Point (`src/validate-yaml.ts`)\n\nThis module handles all I/O concerns, following the pattern of `src/index.ts`.\n\n**Responsibilities:**\n\n1. **Argument parsing**: Extract the file path from `process.argv[2]`.\n2. **Missing argument check**: If no argument, print usage to stderr and exit with code 2.\n3. **File existence check**: Use `Bun.file(path).exists()` to verify the file is accessible. If not, print error to stderr and exit with code 2.\n4. **File reading**: Use `Bun.file(path).text()` to read file contents as a string.\n5. **Validation delegation**: Call `validateYaml(content)` from the validation module.\n6. **Output formatting**:\n   - Success: Print `\"Valid YAML: <filepath>\"` to stdout.\n   - Failure: Print `\"Invalid YAML: <filepath>\"` followed by error details to stderr.\n7. **Exit code management**: Call `process.exit()` with the appropriate code.\n\n**Exit Code Scheme:**\n\n| Code | Meaning |\n|------|---------|\n| 0 | YAML file is syntactically valid |\n| 1 | YAML file has syntax errors |\n| 2 | Usage error (missing argument, file not found) |\n\n**Output Examples:**\n\n```\n# Success\n$ bun run validate-yaml config.yaml\nValid YAML: config.yaml\n\n# Validation failure\n$ bun run validate-yaml bad.yaml\nInvalid YAML: bad.yaml\n  Error: unexpected end of the stream within a flow collection at line 3, column 1\n\n# File not found\n$ bun run validate-yaml nonexistent.yaml\nError: File not found: nonexistent.yaml\n\n# Missing argument\n$ bun run validate-yaml\nUsage: bun run validate-yaml <file>\n\n  Validate a YAML file for syntactic correctness.\n\n  Arguments:\n    file  Path to the YAML file to validate\n\n  Exit codes:\n    0  Valid YAML\n    1  Invalid YAML (syntax error)\n    2  Usage error (missing argument, file not found)\n```\n\n### 3.3 Package.json Script\n\nAdd a new script entry:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"scripts\": {\n    \"validate-yaml\": \"bun run src/validate-yaml.ts\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThis follows the existing pattern of the `\"dev\"` script which runs `bun run src/index.ts`.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Technology Stack\n\n| Category | Choice | Rationale |\n|----------|--------|-----------|\n| Runtime | Bun | Existing project runtime |\n| Language | TypeScript (strict) | Existing project language |\n| Module system | ESM | Existing project config (`\"type\": \"module\"`) |\n| YAML parsing | Bun built-in YAML parser | Zero-dependency constraint; runtime assumption from requirements |\n| File I/O | `Bun.file()` API | Idiomatic Bun file access |\n| Testing | `bun:test` | Existing test framework |\n| CLI args | `process.argv` | Existing pattern in `src/index.ts` |\n\n---\n\n## 5. Implementation Plan\n\n| ID | Component | Description | Files |\n|----|-----------|-------------|-------|\n| T1 | Validation Logic | Core YAML validation module with pure functions and types | `src/yaml-validator.ts` |\n| T2 | CLI Entry Point | Argument parsing, file I/O, output formatting, exit codes | `src/validate-yaml.ts` |\n| T3 | Unit Tests | Tests for validation logic (valid YAML, invalid YAML, error extraction) | `src/yaml-validator.test.ts` |\n| T4 | Script Wiring | Add `validate-yaml` script to `package.json` | `package.json` |\n\n---\n\n## 6. Implementation DAG\n\n**Parallel Groups** (tasks with no inter-dependencies):\n\n1. [T1] - Foundation module with no dependencies on other new components\n2. [T2, T3] - Both depend only on T1; CLI entry point and tests are independent of each other\n3. [T4] - Wiring depends on CLI entry point existing\n\n**Dependencies**:\n\n- T2 -> T1 (Interface: T2 imports `validateYaml` and `YamlValidationResult` from T1)\n- T3 -> T1 (Interface: T3 imports `validateYaml` from T1 to test it)\n- T4 -> T2 (Build: package.json script references `src/validate-yaml.ts`)\n\n**Critical Path**: T1 -> T2 -> T4\n\n---\n\n## 7. Testing Strategy\n\n### Test Value Assessment\n\n| Test Category | Valuable? | Rationale |\n|---------------|-----------|-----------|\n| Valid YAML returns success result | Yes | App-specific business logic |\n| Invalid YAML returns failure with error details | Yes | App-specific error handling |\n| Error message extraction (line/column) | Yes | App-specific data transform |\n| Empty string handling | Yes | App-specific edge case |\n| CLI argument parsing | Yes | App-specific integration behavior |\n| File not found behavior | Yes | App-specific error handling |\n| Exit code correctness | Yes | App-specific contract |\n| Bun YAML parser itself parses YAML correctly | No | Library behavior verification -- do not test |\n\n### Unit Tests (`src/yaml-validator.test.ts`)\n\nTests for the `validateYaml` function:\n\n| Test | Input | Expected Output | Traces To |\n|------|-------|-----------------|-----------|\n| Valid simple YAML returns valid result | `\"key: value\"` | `{ valid: true }` | FR-001 AC-1 |\n| Valid multi-line YAML returns valid result | Multi-line YAML string | `{ valid: true }` | FR-001 AC-1 |\n| Invalid YAML returns invalid result with error | `\"key: [invalid\"` | `{ valid: false, error: { message: \"...\" } }` | FR-001 AC-2, FR-002 |\n| Invalid YAML error includes line info when available | YAML with known error position | `error.line` is a number | FR-002 AC-2 |\n| Empty string is valid YAML | `\"\"` | `{ valid: true }` | FR-001 AC-1 |\n\n### Integration/CLI Tests\n\nThese can be tested via `bun run src/validate-yaml.ts` in a subprocess or by testing the CLI module's behavior directly:\n\n| Test | Scenario | Expected | Traces To |\n|------|----------|----------|-----------|\n| Valid file exits 0 | Run with valid YAML fixture | Exit code 0, success message on stdout | FR-001, FR-006 AC-1 |\n| Invalid file exits 1 | Run with invalid YAML fixture | Exit code 1, error on stderr | FR-001, FR-002, FR-006 AC-2 |\n| Missing file exits 2 | Run with nonexistent path | Exit code 2, file-not-found on stderr | FR-003, FR-006 AC-3 |\n| No args exits 2 | Run with no arguments | Exit code 2, usage message on stderr | FR-004, FR-006 AC-3 |\n\n---\n\n## 8. Deployment Design\n\nThis feature requires no special deployment steps. It is a local CLI command added to the project.\n\n| Aspect | Detail |\n|--------|--------|\n| Build | No build step required; Bun runs TypeScript directly |\n| Distribution | Available immediately via `bun run validate-yaml` after code is merged |\n| Rollback | Revert the commit; remove script entry and source files |\n| Environment requirements | Bun runtime (already required by the project) |\n| Configuration | None required |\n\n---\n\n## 9. Documentation Impact\n\n| Type | Target | Section | KB Source | Rationale |\n|------|--------|---------|-----------|-----------|\n| edit | `README.md` | Scripts | N/A (no KB) | Add `validate-yaml` to the list of available scripts |\n| edit | `README.md` | Structure | N/A (no KB) | Add new files to the project structure listing |\n\n---\n\n## 10. Design Decisions Log\n\n| ID | Decision | Choice | Rationale | Alternatives Considered |\n|----|----------|--------|-----------|------------------------|\n| D1 | Module separation | Separate validation logic from CLI entry point | Follows existing `utils.ts`/`index.ts` pattern; enables unit testing of pure logic without I/O | Single file with everything inline |\n| D2 | YAML parsing approach | Bun built-in YAML parser | Zero-dependency constraint; requirement assumes Bun provides this | Write custom YAML tokenizer (complex, error-prone); use `js-yaml` (violates no-new-deps) |\n| D3 | File I/O API | `Bun.file()` API | Idiomatic Bun; async-friendly; avoids importing `fs` | Node.js `fs.readFileSync` (works but less idiomatic) |\n| D4 | Exit code scheme | 0=success, 1=validation error, 2=usage error | Standard CLI convention per FR-006; distinguishes error categories per BR-003 | Single non-zero code for all errors |\n| D5 | Output destinations | stdout for success, stderr for errors | Standard Unix convention; allows piping stdout without error noise | All output to stdout |\n| D6 | Test strategy | Unit tests on validation module; integration-style tests on CLI flow | Maximizes value; pure function tests are fast and reliable | Only integration tests (slower, harder to isolate) |\n| D7 | File naming | `validate-yaml.ts`, `yaml-validator.ts` | kebab-case matching existing file naming convention; entry point named after command, module named after its role | camelCase files (inconsistent with project) |\n"
