        "content": "# Design Decisions: YAML Validator Command\n\n**Feature ID**: yaml-validator-command\n**Created**: 2026-03-01\n\n## Decision Log\n\n| ID | Decision | Choice | Rationale | Alternatives Considered |\n|----|----------|--------|-----------|------------------------|\n| D1 | Module separation strategy | Separate validation logic (`yaml-validator.ts`) from CLI entry point (`validate-yaml.ts`) | Follows existing `utils.ts`/`index.ts` separation pattern; enables unit testing of pure validation logic without I/O side effects | Single monolithic file with all logic inline |\n| D2 | YAML parsing approach | Bun built-in YAML parser | Zero-dependency hard constraint (FR-005, BR-001); requirement assumes Bun runtime provides this capability | Write custom YAML tokenizer (complex, error-prone, likely incomplete); add `js-yaml` package (violates no-new-deps constraint) |\n| D3 | File I/O API | `Bun.file()` API for file existence checking and reading | Idiomatic Bun API; provides clean `exists()` and `text()` methods; avoids importing Node.js `fs` module | `node:fs` readFileSync/existsSync (works in Bun but less idiomatic); raw `fetch()` with file:// protocol |\n| D4 | Exit code scheme | 0 = success, 1 = validation error, 2 = usage error | Standard CLI convention aligned with FR-006; distinguishes error categories as required by BR-003 | Single non-zero code (1) for all errors (less informative for scripting); Only 0 and 1 (cannot distinguish error types) |\n| D5 | Output stream routing | Success messages to stdout, error messages to stderr | Standard Unix convention; enables piping valid output without error noise; supports both human and machine consumption | All output to stdout (mixes success and error streams); JSON output (over-engineered for the use case) |\n| D6 | Test approach | Unit tests for pure validation functions; separate integration tests for CLI behavior | Maximizes test value by isolating business logic from I/O; fast unit tests with high coverage; integration tests verify end-to-end contract | Only integration tests via subprocess (slower, harder to debug); Only unit tests (misses CLI wiring issues) |\n| D7 | File naming convention | `validate-yaml.ts` (entry point), `yaml-validator.ts` (module), `yaml-validator.test.ts` (tests) | kebab-case matching existing project convention (`index.ts`, `utils.ts`, `utils.test.ts`); entry point named after the command; module named after its role | camelCase filenames (inconsistent with existing project); PascalCase (inconsistent with project) |\n| D8 | Script name in package.json | `validate-yaml` | kebab-case matching CLI convention; descriptive and clear; `bun run validate-yaml` reads naturally | `validate:yaml` (colon-separated namespacing, not used in existing scripts); `yaml-validate` (less natural verb-noun order) |\n| D9 | Result type design | `YamlValidationResult` interface with optional `error` object containing `message`, `line?`, `column?` | Structured return type enables clean conditional handling; optional line/column accommodates parsers that may not provide position info | Throw on invalid (caller must catch; less explicit); Return tuple `[boolean, string]` (less structured) |\n| D10 | Argument parsing approach | Direct `process.argv` indexing | Follows exact pattern in existing `src/index.ts`; single required argument does not warrant a parsing library | `util.parseArgs` (over-engineered for single positional arg); Third-party arg parser (violates no-new-deps) |\n\n## AFK Mode: Auto-Selected Technology Decisions\n\n| Decision | Choice | Source | Rationale |\n|----------|--------|--------|-----------|\n| Runtime | Bun | Codebase (`package.json` scripts) | Project already uses Bun as its runtime |\n| Language | TypeScript (strict mode) | Codebase (`tsconfig.json`) | Project is TypeScript with strict mode enabled |\n| Module system | ESM | Codebase (`package.json` \"type\": \"module\") | Project uses ES modules |\n| Test framework | `bun:test` | Codebase (`src/utils.test.ts`) | Project already uses `bun:test` with describe/test/expect pattern |\n| File I/O API | `Bun.file()` | Codebase pattern (idiomatic Bun) | Most idiomatic Bun API for file operations |\n| Argument parsing | `process.argv` | Codebase (`src/index.ts`) | Existing pattern for CLI argument access |\n| YAML parser | Bun built-in | Requirement assumption (Section 4.3) | Requirements assume Bun provides built-in YAML parsing; zero-dependency constraint prohibits alternatives |\n| Output format | Plain text to stdout/stderr | Conservative default | Standard CLI convention; no requirement suggests structured output |\n| Error detail level | Message + line/column when available | Requirements (FR-002) | Requirements specify actionable error reporting with position information |\n"
      },
