        "content": "# Hypothesis Document: yaml-validator-command\n**Version**: 1.0.0 | **Created**: 2026-03-01T01:26:00Z | **Status**: VALIDATED\n\n## Hypotheses\n### HYP-001: Bun runtime provides built-in YAML parsing capability\n**Risk Level**: HIGH\n**Status**: CONFIRMED\n**Statement**: Bun runtime provides a built-in YAML parsing capability that can be used without adding external dependencies.\n**Context**: The entire design depends on a zero-dependency YAML parser being available in Bun. If this is not available, the feature cannot be implemented as designed without adding an external library (violating FR-005/BR-001).\n**Validation Criteria**:\n- CONFIRM if: Bun exposes a YAML parse function (e.g., `Bun.YAML.parse`) that can parse a YAML string into a JavaScript object without any npm package installation.\n- REJECT if: No built-in YAML parsing capability exists in Bun and an external package is required.\n**Suggested Method**: CODE_EXPERIMENT\n\n### HYP-002: Bun's built-in YAML parser provides line/column position in parse errors\n**Risk Level**: MEDIUM\n**Status**: REJECTED\n**Statement**: Bun's built-in YAML parser provides line and column position information in parse error exceptions, enabling actionable error reporting.\n**Context**: FR-002 requires that error output include line number or position information \"when the underlying parser provides it.\" The design assumes the parser may provide this, with the `YamlValidationResult.error` type including optional `line?` and `column?` fields.\n**Validation Criteria**:\n- CONFIRM if: The `SyntaxError` thrown by `Bun.YAML.parse()` on invalid input contains YAML-source line and/or column information, either as structured properties or embedded in the error message.\n- REJECT if: The error object contains only generic messages with no YAML-source position information, or the `line`/`column` properties refer to the JavaScript call site rather than the YAML content.\n**Suggested Method**: CODE_EXPERIMENT\n\n## Validation Findings\n\n### HYP-001 Findings\n**Validated**: 2026-03-01T01:27:00Z\n**Method**: CODE_EXPERIMENT + EXTERNAL_RESEARCH\n**Result**: CONFIRMED\n\n**Evidence**:\nBun 1.3.3 provides a built-in `Bun.YAML` object with `parse` and `stringify` methods. No npm package installation is required. The parser is written in Zig and currently passes over 90% of the official YAML test suite.\n\nExperimental code confirmed:\n1. `Bun.YAML` exists as an object on the Bun global with two methods: `parse` and `stringify`.\n2. `Bun.YAML.parse(\"key: value\\nlist:\\n  - item1\\n  - item2\")` successfully returns `{ key: \"value\", list: [\"item1\", \"item2\"] }`.\n3. The parser throws a `SyntaxError` on invalid YAML input (e.g., `\"key: [unclosed\"`).\n4. `Bun.YAML` appears alongside `Bun.TOML` as a built-in parser in the Bun namespace.\n5. No `import` or `require` of any external package is needed.\n\nFull list of Bun namespace properties was enumerated; `YAML` is present as a first-class built-in alongside `TOML`, `SQL`, `S3Client`, `Glob`, and other Bun-native APIs.\n\n**Sources**:\n- Code experiment: `/tmp/hypothesis-yaml-validator-command/hyp001-builtin-yaml.ts` (disposable)\n- Official docs: https://bun.com/docs/api/yaml\n- API reference: https://bun.com/reference/bun/YAML\n- Bun YAML runtime docs: https://bun.com/docs/runtime/yaml\n\n**Implications for Design**:\nThe design's core assumption (D2: \"Bun built-in YAML parser\") is valid. `Bun.YAML.parse(content)` can be used directly in `src/yaml-validator.ts` to implement the `validateYaml` function. The zero-dependency constraint (FR-005, BR-001) can be satisfied. No changes to the design are needed for this aspect.\n\n---\n\n### HYP-002 Findings\n**Validated**: 2026-03-01T01:28:00Z\n**Method**: CODE_EXPERIMENT + EXTERNAL_RESEARCH\n**Result**: REJECTED\n\n**Evidence**:\nBun's `Bun.YAML.parse()` throws a `SyntaxError` on invalid YAML, but the error object does NOT contain YAML-source line/column position information. The `line` and `column` properties on the error refer to the **JavaScript/TypeScript source file call site**, not the position within the YAML string.\n\nExperimental proof:\n1. The error `line`/`column` values change based on where `Bun.YAML.parse()` is called in the TypeScript file, NOT based on where the error is in the YAML string:\n   - Calling from TS line ~38: `e.line = 40`\n   - Calling from TS line ~43: `e.line = 45`\n   - Same invalid YAML input in both cases.\n\n2. Different YAML inputs with errors at different positions all produce identical `line`/`column` values when parsed from the same call site:\n   - `\"[unclosed\"` (error at YAML line 1) -> `line: 17, column: 14`\n   - `\"a: 1\\nb: 2\\nc: [unclosed\"` (error at YAML line 3) -> `line: 17, column: 14`\n   - 11-line YAML with error on line 11 -> `line: 17, column: 14`\n   All point to the same TypeScript source location.\n\n3. The error message is always the generic string `\"YAML Parse error: Unexpected token\"` with no YAML position information embedded. Regex check for `line|col|pos|offset|row|char` in the message returns false for all test cases.\n\n4. The `sourceURL` property confirms this: it points to the TypeScript file path, not the YAML content.\n\n5. No additional properties exist on the error object beyond: `message`, `originalLine`, `originalColumn`, `line`, `column`, `sourceURL`, `stack`. The `originalLine`/`originalColumn` also refer to the JavaScript source, not YAML content.\n\n6. GitHub issue #22659 (oven-sh/bun) confirms the community has observed that Bun's YAML error reporting provides minimal diagnostic information compared to libraries like js-yaml. The fix in PR #23265 focused on parsing correctness (90% test suite pass rate), not error reporting quality.\n\n7. The official Bun API documentation at bun.com/docs/api/yaml does not mention any line/column position information in parse errors.\n\n**Sources**:\n- Code experiments: `/tmp/hypothesis-yaml-validator-command/hyp002-error-positions.ts`, `hyp002-deep-test.ts`, `hyp002-final.ts` (disposable)\n- GitHub issue: https://github.com/oven-sh/bun/issues/22659\n- Official docs: https://bun.com/docs/api/yaml\n- API reference: https://bun.com/reference/bun/YAML/parse\n\n**Implications for Design**:\n1. The `YamlValidationResult.error` type with optional `line?` and `column?` fields remains structurally sound (they are already optional), but these fields will **never be populated** with the current Bun version (1.3.3).\n2. FR-002 AC-2 states error output should include position info \"when the underlying parser provides it.\" Since the parser does NOT provide it, the acceptance criterion is technically met by omission -- but the user experience will be degraded compared to what the design envisions.\n3. The error message will always be the generic \"YAML Parse error: Unexpected token\" which provides limited diagnostic value for developers trying to locate errors in their YAML files.\n4. The design should acknowledge this limitation. Options for the implementer:\n   - Accept the generic error message as-is (simplest, stays within constraints).\n   - Parse the error message for any future improvements Bun may add.\n   - Consider whether a fallback approach (e.g., a simple line-by-line heuristic) could improve error reporting without adding dependencies.\n5. The output examples in the design (Section 3.2) showing `\"unexpected end of the stream within a flow collection at line 3, column 1\"` are **not achievable** with the current Bun YAML parser. The actual output will be `\"YAML Parse error: Unexpected token\"` with no position details.\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n| Hypothesis | Risk | Result | Implication |\n|------------|------|--------|-------------|\n| HYP-001: Bun has built-in YAML parsing | HIGH | CONFIRMED | Core design assumption is valid; `Bun.YAML.parse()` works without dependencies |\n| HYP-002: Parse errors include line/column info | MEDIUM | REJECTED | Error position info unavailable; design